re's a lot  of things that Luce does in the film  that I just had I was wondering why  because I'm just not a I'm not a fighter  per se that sounds bad but I always tell  people if I were to draw and I'll  probably just float and just close my  eyes and rest but Lewis is not that guy  he's gonna swim and so I think I just  was like why is he fighting so hard for  this and he kind of he kind of basically  told me I told him some stories about my  high school experience and growing up  and that's some of the racial race  issues I faced and he was  like they're very similar and it's not  that far from you so it was just trying  to to humanize the 17 year old boy who  is a human being that I just for some  reason put in a box too you know and I  was wondering if there's any difference  in terms of the different generations  represented in the movie specifically  for the adults and then these teenagers  so can you two speak a little bit to  that point well I think we grew up in a  generation where our parents set the  tone and said this is who you're going  to be this is the path that we want you  to go on and we kind of just accepted  that we're now in a new time where the  kids are saying you know what no we we  are actually smart people we can trust  ourselves we can shape our identities  and we don't need you interfering and I  think this is what this film is about it  happens with both Luce Kelvin and and  Stephanie's character Stephanie not yeah  and they're pushing back and saying  we're in charge of our identity and  please don't put your agenda onto us can  you tell us a little bit about your  character Oh feel free I love the  challenge of having so many people and I  want to get everybody a little bit of  time here can you tell me a little bit  about your characters relationship to  loose and how your experience throughout  the movie differs from his yeah I play I  guess I don't want to give away too much  but I play Lucis classmate and I  actually feel like my character  Stephanie is quite similar to loose out  of all the characters which is Stephanie  kind of has the ability to shed a  different light on Lucis character which  is super important to the movie and  Marsha where do you step in here I play  Octavia's sister Harriet sister Rosemary  and I think I'm there to sort of throw a  little monkey wrench into her somewhat  very steady life that she's established  for herself and so between Luce and I  I think we kind of just mess it up a  little bit can you talk a little bit  about working with a Octavia cuz she is  one of the people in this industry that  I admire greatly and I love seeing all  the different projects she chooses  because she chooses them with such value  and purpose so I just have a great  respect for her I I mean she's one of my  heroes obviously and I was really happy  the first day we worked together she we  were in the car and they had mentioned  something technical that I I didn't know  what it was but you know as a new person  on set you don't want to ask anyone who  seemed like you don't know and I  remember her turning to me and be like  I'm sorry she's she's so disused I have  no idea what this thing is they keep  mentioning it and I was like oh my god  me too and so I loved her tim is the  only one who hasn't answered a question  here I'm gonna give this one to you okay  the movie taps into the feeling of being  an outsider and I find that anybody who  feels that way out there can only  benefit from hearing somebody else's  experience if they've gone through  something like that so have you ever  been able to kind of connect to what  some of the characters in this movie are  experiencing and how did you pull  yourself out of it only in the I came  from England to hear on that front and  also doing a fake job so there's that  element but no we've talked about it  we've taught it's that what what we are  our characters who are in feel that  they're in a comfortable in a company in  the comfort zone that they have they  have they are quite liberal and they're  very engaged and they think they feel  that they're doing the right thing they  don't see any any racism within  themselves and that's what we found to  be interesting for us to examine it and  I opposed the outsider question now to  the group because again I think it's an  important conversation to have so is  there any feeling of being able to  relate to any of these characters in any  capacity and maybe if you haven't - just  being steeped in this story for the  shooting period sometimes you can  realise new truths about yourselves  or about somebody else that you might  not have been able to process before I  think everyone feels like an outsider  and anyone who doesn't is an outsider I  feel like it would it be weird like we  all feel like oh my god I wish I wish I  am people understood I wish I wasn't the  only crazy one I wish I wasn't the only  one or the only I think everyone can  relate to that on some level otherwise  they're fakes it's very true  I was wondering a little bit about the  process for you of going from something  like Cloverfield paradox which is a  completely different type of movie here  that a lot of our viewers are very well  aware of - doing something like this  just the importance of being able to do  a big crazy action movie like that but  then also having something like this  that really says something and can make  a big difference in someone's life well  this is actually where I started I mean  I studied theater for my first degree  and I went to film school and these were  the kind of projects I made Cloverfield  was an interesting opportunity to came  my way and but this is kind of my heart  and soul and what I'm truly truly  passionate about so I'm grateful to have  opportunities to do some of the larger  things but I think things like these are  so necessarily important and again just  getting a chance to work with all these  actors it's this is a dream come 